// A submodule, and the file that shows what a submodule does NOT reach.
//
// A module's namespace holds what it declares, what a fold brought in, and its own
// children. It does not hold its sisters: 'log' is a module of this module's
// parent, and nothing binds that name here, so naming it is a name that does not
// resolve rather than a name that is refused.
//
// WHAT WOULD BIND IT IS AN IMPORT, and this file deliberately writes none. A
// module is the registry its children resolve against, so 'import log;' here
// finds her in the parent's namespace and binds her name -- the same mechanism
// that will reach an external package, asking a different registry. What is
// pinned here is that the registry is read on request: a sister is a dependency
// a module states, not one it is handed. module_import_sister states one.

mod store;

pub fn keep(n i64) i64 {
  // Its own child is in reach, because a child IS a name of this namespace
  cache.hold(n);
}

fn hidden(n i64) i64 {
  // A sister of this module, which this module cannot see
  log.write(n);               //~ ErrorUnkName "does not refer to a declared name"
}
